[Event "Kings Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/3snUMYzJ"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we play the Kings Gambit. Give away a pawn, take the whole centre. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Straight to the middle. Now he has to meet you there. } (1. d4 { A great move, and a whole different world. } 1... Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 { That is the queen pawn game. Today we want the open one. }) 1... e5 { He copies you. Time for the gambit: push the f pawn two squares. [%cal Yf2f4] } 2. f4 { The Kings Gambit. You offer a pawn to blow his centre pawn away. [%csl Re5] } (2. Nf3 { Solid, and a real weapon in its own right. But it is not our adventure today. } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 { That is the Spanish, and a very long story. }) (2. Bc4 { A fine developing move. But the bishop can wait one turn. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d3 Bb4 { A normal quiet opening. Sound, and much calmer. }) (2. Nc3 { Also perfectly good. But the f pawn is the whole point today. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 { A four knights game. Sound, and a different study. }) 2... exf4 { He grabs it. The gambit is accepted. Bring the king knight out. [%cal Yg1f3] } 3. Nf3 { The knight covers h4, so his queen can never check you there. [%csl Rh4] } (3. Bc4 { The bishops gambit, and just as good. But it invites a check. } 3... c6 4. Nc3 Qh4+ 5. Kf1 d5 { Playable, but your king cannot castle. }) (3. Nc3 { Also fine, a natural developing move. But the knight belongs on f3, covering the check. } 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 g5 5. h4 g4 { You reach our line anyway, one move later. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing the centre looks natural. But the check comes with tempo. } 3... Qh4+ 4. Ke2 Qe7 5. Kf2 Nf6 { Your king is stuck on f2. Not what we want. }) 3... g5 { He builds a pawn wall to hold f4. Poke it: push the rook pawn to h4. [%cal Yh2h4] } 4. h4 { You knock on the wall. If he takes, the h file opens at his king. [%csl Rg5] } (4. d4 { A very good move, grabbing the centre while you can. But we want to break the wall. } 4... d6 5. g3 g4 6. Nh4 f3 { Sound and slower. Our rook pawn is more fun. }) (4. Nc3 { Also completely fine, and quieter. } 4... d6 5. d4 g4 6. Bxf4 gxf3 7. Qxf3 { A good gambit as well. But our rook pawn is sharper. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting, the bishop stares at f7. But the g pawn keeps rolling. } 4... g4 5. O-O gxf3 6. Qxf3 Qf6 { A wild gambit. Fun, but you are a piece down. }) 4... g4 { The pawn runs past. Do not lose the knight: jump it in to e5. [%cal Yf3e5] } 5. Ne5 { Well posted. Your knight is safe on e5 and it stares straight at f7. [%cal Ge5f7] } (5. d4 { Look again: nothing at all is guarding your knight on f3. } 5... gxf3 6. Qxf3 Nc6 7. c3 d6 { A whole piece for one pawn. The queen cannot fix that. }) (5. Ng5 { The knight jumps at f7. Bold, but he simply chases it away. } 5... h6 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. d4 f3 { Two pawns for a knight is not enough. }) (5. Ng1 { Home again, safe and sad. Two moves thrown away. } 5... d5 6. d4 Bh6 7. e5 c5 { He has the centre and the extra pawn. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Kieseritzky main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/xYOugfFE"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_5"]

{ Your knight sits deep in his position on e5. Watch what he tries against it. }
5... Nf6 { He develops and takes aim at your e pawn. What now? } 6. d4 { Nice. You back the knight up and take the whole centre. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (6. Nxg4 { A good grab in its own right. But we want the big centre instead. } 6... Nc6 7. Nc3 Nd4 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Level and tidy, with no attack left. }) (6. Bc4 { Perfectly playable too. But the knight on e5 gets no help. } 6... d5 7. exd5 Bd6 8. d4 Nh5 { He gains time and the pawn on f4 holds. }) (6. Be2 { Too shy. The knight in the middle still stands alone. } 6... Nxe4 7. d4 Ng3 8. Rh2 d6 { His knight is a monster and yours must run. }) 6... d6 { He kicks your knight. Where does it go so it still does a job? } 7. Nd3 { Well spotted. From d3 the knight is safe and it leans on f4. [%cal Gd3f4] } (7. Bxf4 { Look again: what is holding your knight on e5? } 7... dxe5 8. Bxe5 Nbd7 9. Bf4 Nh5 { A whole piece for one pawn. }) (7. Nxf7 { The classic sacrifice. Here it is just a knight for two pawns. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxf4 Be6 9. Qd2 Rg8 { His king is fine and you are a piece short. }) (7. Nc4 { The knight steps aside, but c4 does nothing at all. } 7... Bh6 8. Nc3 Nh5 9. Bd3 O-O { He castles by hand and you have no plan. }) 7... Nxe4 { He grabs a pawn on e4. What is the best way to win one back? } 8. Bxf4 { Lovely. The bishop takes back and leans on his d6 pawn. [%cal Gf4d6] } (8. Qe2 { Also good, the queen eyes the e file. But the bishop wants f4. } 8... Qe7 9. Bxf4 Nc6 10. c3 Nf6 { Fine for you, just one move slower. }) (8. Nxf4 { The wrong piece takes. Your knight leaves and the bishop is still asleep. } 8... Be7 9. Bd3 Bxh4+ { The h pawn drops with check. The bishop had to take on f4. }) (8. Nc3 { Development, but it lets his knight leap to g3. } 8... Ng3 9. Rh2 Qe7+ 10. Kf2 Bg7 { Your king is stuck in the middle. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he hits back with the d pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/rbS1fAhY"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Not everyone grabs the f pawn. Some players hit back in the centre instead. }
2... d5 { He skips the pawn grab and strikes in the middle with his d pawn. Take it, or let him keep a big centre for free? } 3. exd5 { Yes. Take it. Never let him keep a big centre for free. } (3. Nf3 { Also excellent, and often the tidier choice. It usually comes to the same thing. } 3... exf4 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Bc4 Nxd5 { You have simply transposed into our line. }) (3. fxe5 { Look again: his queen has a road all the way to h4. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook is gone. Take on d5 instead. }) (3. Nc3 { Playable, but he pushes past and cramps you. } 3... d4 4. Nce2 Nc6 5. d3 exf4 { His pawn wedge is annoying to face. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing more centre, but his pieces come flying out. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 { Your queen gets chased and he is faster. }) 3... exf4 { Now he takes the f pawn back. Which piece wants to come out first? } 4. Nf3 { Good. The knight comes out and covers h4, so no queen checks. [%csl Rh4] } (4. Bb5+ { A tempting check. But he blocks and gains time on your bishop. } 4... c6 5. Qe2+ Be7 6. dxc6 bxc6 { His pieces come out with tempo. }) (4. d4 { The big centre looks right. But the check on h4 is still open. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kd2 Qd8 6. Nc3 Nf6 { Your king has moved and you are worse. }) (4. Qf3 { The queen guards nothing useful out there. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Bd6 6. Bb5+ c6 { She will be chased and you are behind. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting: the bishop stares straight at f7. But the check comes first. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kf1 { Playable, but now you can never castle. The knight move avoided all of this. }) 4... Nf6 { He goes after your extra pawn on d5. Can you keep it? } 5. c4 { Yes. The little c pawn props d5 up and your centre holds. [%cal Gc4d5] } (5. Bc4 { A good move too. You give the pawn back and develop quickly. } 5... c6 6. d4 cxd5 7. Bb5+ Nc6 { A fine game, but the extra pawn is gone. }) (5. Bb5+ { Also perfectly good. The check buys a moment to castle. } 5... c6 6. dxc6 Nxc6 7. Nc3 Bb4 { Equal and pleasant. But we prefer to keep the pawn. }) (5. Nc3 { Natural, but now the pawn on d5 falls for free. } 5... Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 7. d4 Be7 { The pawn is gone and his queen sits in the middle. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he declines with the bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRvxW008"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Not everyone grabs pawns. Some players develop instead and dare you to prove the gambit is real. }
2... Bc5 { He turns the pawn down and points his bishop at f2. Be careful here. } 3. Nf3 { Right. The knight covers h4, so his bishop has no check to go with it. [%csl Rh4] } (3. fxe5 { Greedy, and it opens a straight road to your king. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook is gone. That is why the knight goes first. }) (3. Nc3 { A good move as well, and just as sound. } 3... d6 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bc4 O-O { You reach the same kind of set up anyway. }) (3. d3 { Solid, but slow, and the check on h4 is still in the air. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 a5 5. Nf3 Nf6 { Playable, but he is very comfortable. }) 3... d6 { He props his pawn up with d6. What is your plan against that bishop? } 4. c3 { Nice. The little pawn move prepares d4, and d4 kicks the bishop. [%cal Gc3d4] } (4. Nc3 { Also good. Plain development, and nothing wrong with it. } 4... Nf6 5. Bc4 O-O 6. d3 Nbd7 { A normal game. But we want to hit that bishop. }) (4. Bc4 { Perfectly playable, and the bishops stare at each other. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 O-O 6. d3 Nbd7 { Even and quiet. Our pawn plan is sharper. }) (4. fxe5 { You can take. But it just helps him develop for free. } 4... dxe5 5. c3 f5 6. Bc4 Nc6 { Equal, and the tension is gone. }) 4... Nc6 { Knight to c6. Your last move was preparing something. Play it. } 5. d4 { There it is. The pawn hits his bishop and you own the middle. [%cal Gd4c5] } (5. fxe5 { You can take here too, and it stays about level. } 5... dxe5 6. b4 f5 7. exf5 Bb6 { Playable. But the centre push was the point of c3. }) (5. b4 { The pawn grabs space and hits the bishop. Not bad at all. } 5... Bb6 6. a4 a5 7. b5 Nce7 { Fine for you, but your queen side loosens. }) (5. Bb5 { The pin looks natural, but he hits back with the f pawn. } 5... f5 6. d4 exd4 7. cxd4 Bb4+ { Messy, and your bishop had to move twice. }) 5... exd4 { He takes. Two of your men can take back. Only one is right. } 6. cxd4 { Exactly. The c pawn takes, his bishop is kicked, and the centre is yours. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. Nxd4 { The knight takes, but the c pawn wanted that job. } 6... Nf6 7. Bd3 Qe7 8. Nd2 O-O { His pieces flow and your big centre never happened. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop looks nice, but you left the pawn on d4 alone. } 6... dxc3 7. Nxc3 Nf6 8. e5 dxe5 { Your big centre is gone and he is better. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Fischer defence"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/dU5DOQyZ"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_3"]

{ No pawn wall today. This is the Fischer defence — meeting your gambit calmly instead of grabbing. }
3... d6 { The calm d6, keeping everything solid. No weaknesses for you to attack yet. } 4. Bc4 { Good pick. The bishop flies out and glares straight at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] } (4. d4 { Also strong. You take the whole centre instead. } 4... g5 5. Qd3 Bg7 6. g3 Nc6 { A good game too. But the bishop wanted f7. }) (4. h4 { Stopping his pawn push early. Perfectly playable. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. d4 Nh5 { Sound, but your bishop is still sitting at home. }) (4. Nc3 { Fine, but it lets him build the wall with the g pawn. } 4... g5 5. Bc4 g4 6. O-O gxf3 7. Qxf3 { A wild gambit, and you are a piece down. }) 4... h6 { He plays h6, getting ready to push the g pawn. Can you stop that? } 5. h4 { Yes. Now if the g pawn comes you take twice and your rook grabs h8. [%csl Rg5] } (5. d4 { Also good, and very natural. But he gets the wall in anyway. } 5... g5 6. h4 Bg7 7. Qd3 Nc6 { Sound. You just play the rook pawn a move later. }) (5. Nc3 { Fine development. But now the g pawn comes with tempo. } 5... g5 6. h4 Bg7 7. d4 Nc6 { Playable, and a little less pleasant. }) (5. O-O { Castling is safe and sensible. But the wall goes up for free. } 5... g5 6. c3 Nc6 7. d4 Nf6 { He keeps the pawn and you have no attack. }) 5... Nf6 { Knight to f6, hitting your e pawn. Who should look after it? } 6. Nc3 { Perfect. The knight guards e4, and it eyes d5 too. [%cal Gc3e4] } (6. d4 { Look again: nobody at all is guarding your e pawn. } 6... Nxe4 7. Qe2 Qe7 8. Bxf4 Nf6 { A pawn gone for no reason. }) (6. O-O { Castling is nice, but the e pawn is still hanging. } 6... Nh5 7. d4 g5 8. Nc3 Bg7 { He holds f4 and you have nothing to show. }) (6. d3 { Solid, but slow, and the f4 pawn stays put. } 6... Nh5 7. Nc3 Be7 8. d4 Nc6 { He is comfortable and a pawn up. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the early queen check"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRuK3zgO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ Sometimes he skips development entirely and brings the queen out early instead. }
2... Qh4+ { Out comes the queen with check. Only two moves are legal here — choose well. } 3. g3 { The right one. The pawn attacks her and wins you a move. [%cal Gg3h4] } (3. Ke2 { The king has to walk, and your castling is gone forever. } 3... Bc5 4. Qe1 Qxf4 5. d3 Qf6 { He is a pawn up and your king is naked. }) 3... Qe7 { She backs off to e7, right in front of his own bishop. Your move. } 4. Nc3 { Good. Develop, and leave the pawn there. His queen is in her own way. [%csl Rf8] } (4. d3 { Also good, and very sensible. But the knight is quicker. } 4... d5 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. dxe4 Nc6 { Pleasant for you either way. }) (4. Bg2 { Fine too, though it does less than the knight. } 4... d5 5. Nc3 dxe4 6. fxe5 Qxe5 { Still nice for you, just a shade slower. }) (4. c4 { Space, and perfectly playable. But develop the pieces first. } 4... exf4 5. Nc3 fxg3 6. hxg3 d6 { The same idea, one move later. }) 4... exf4 { At last he takes the f pawn. What is your strongest push now? } 5. d4 { Yes. Two pawns wide, and his queen is stuck behind her own men. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (5. gxf4 { You can take back, but then the middle opens up for him. } 5... d5 6. Qe2 dxe4 7. Nxe4 Nc6 { Your edge has gone. The big push was better. }) (5. Bg2 { A good move too. The bishop takes the long road. } 5... fxg3 6. hxg3 d6 7. d4 g6 { Still comfortable. But the pawn push was stronger. }) (5. Qf3 { The queen comes out early. Playable, but she can be chased. } 5... c6 6. gxf4 d5 7. d3 dxe4 { Fine, and a good deal messier. }) 5... fxg3 { He takes on g3, right beside your king. Which pawn takes back? } 6. hxg3 { Well spotted. The h file swings open and your rook stares down it. [%cal Gh1h7] } (6. Bf4 { Also excellent. Develop first and take the pawn later. } 6... Nf6 7. hxg3 d6 8. Qd3 Nc6 { Just as good. Both roads open the h file. }) (6. Qf3 { Also fine, but that pawn on g3 really deserves taking. } 6... d6 7. Nd5 Qd8 8. Bf4 c6 { Still pleasant for you, just untidy. }) (6. Nh3 { The knight goes to the edge to guard. Too passive. } 6... Nf6 7. Bg2 gxh2 8. Bg5 c6 { His pawn crawls to h2 and your edge is gone. }) 1-0
